🚽 Test case: issue #311 -- a struct/union-typed ARRAY field inside a
🚽 struct (`gang Pool { gang Entity es[8]; };`).
🚽
🚽 The SCALAR-element counterpart (`chad params[4];`, #206 3e) lives in
🚽 struct_array_field.brainrot; this file is the struct-element case, and
🚽 the two are deliberately separate fixtures rather than one merged file.
🚽
🚽 A SCALAR array field (`rizz nums[4];`) and an array of structs as a
🚽 VARIABLE (`gang Point pts[3];`) each already worked; their intersection
🚽 was a parse error: "unexpected LBRACKET, expecting SEMICOLON". Only the
🚽 grammar was missing -- get_struct_field_size() already takes a
🚽 VAR_STRUCT field's element size from the nested definition's total_size
🚽 and multiplies by the dimension product when is_array is set, and
🚽 get_struct_field_alignment() already returns the nested alignment while
🚽 correctly ignoring is_array (a C array needs no more alignment than its
🚽 element).
🚽
🚽 ── The sizes below are checked against a C compiler ──────────────────
🚽 Every `gang` here has a matching C struct in
🚽 tests/abi/struct_layout_abi_check.c, whose _Static_assert/offsetof
🚽 establish what a real C compiler produces for the same field lists.
🚽 That file is ground truth for these numbers; this fixture is what
🚽 proves Brainrot agrees with it.
🚽
🚽 ── Why the neighbouring scalar fields matter ─────────────────────────
🚽 An array field lives inline in the enclosing struct's single blob, so a
🚽 wrong element stride does not merely misindex its own array -- it
🚽 reads and writes into whatever field follows. Every struct below keeps
🚽 a scalar field after its array and checks it, so a striding regression
🚽 shows up as corruption of that field rather than only as a wrong
🚽 element value.
gang Entity {
    chad x;
    chad y;
    cap alive;
};

gang Pool {
    gang Entity es[3];
    rizz count;
    chad speed;
};

gang Cell {
    rizz v;
};

gang Grid {
    gang Cell cells[2][3];
    rizz tag;
};

gang Inner {
    chad a;
};

gang Mid {
    gang Inner one;
    gang Inner many[2];
};

gang Outer {
    gang Mid m;
    rizz tag;
};

🚽 A helper that walks the whole pool -- the thing this feature exists
🚽 for. Before it, the loop had to be inlined at every call site because
🚽 there was no struct to hand over.
skibidi scroll_all(gang Pool *p) {
    flex (rizz i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) {
        edgy (p.es[i].alive) {
            p.es[i].x = p.es[i].x - p.speed;
        }
    }
}

🚽 Passing an element BY VALUE, and the rejection that must come with it.
🚽 The fixture originally only ever read THROUGH the new syntax
🚽 (`pool.es[i].x`) and never passed one of these things anywhere, which
🚽 is why it stayed green while both directions were broken: the element
🚽 form was refused and the whole-array form was silently accepted as
🚽 element 0 (PR #315 review). Both are checked below.
chad len2(gang Entity e) {
    bussin e.x * e.x + e.y * e.y;
}

🚽 Two by-value struct parameters, so the two-argument UAF shape has
🚽 somewhere to land: with a call base, argument 2's own call is what
🚽 frees the blob argument 1 still points into.
chad dot(gang Entity a, gang Entity b) {
    bussin a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y;
}

🚽 A pool whose elements are distinguishable, returned BY VALUE so a
🚽 `make_pool().es[i]` base resolves through the pending-return slot.
gang Pool make_pool() {
    gang Pool t;
    t.es[0].x = 3.0;
    t.es[0].y = 4.0;
    t.es[1].x = 6.0;
    t.es[1].y = 8.0;
    t.count = 2;
    t.speed = 0.5;
    bussin t;
}

🚽 The return form: a struct-array-field element returned out of a
🚽 function whose own base was a call.
gang Entity grab() {
    bussin make_pool().es[1];
}

skibidi main {
    gang Pool pool;
    yapping("sizeof Pool: %lu", maxxing(pool));

    pool.speed = 1.5;
    pool.count = 3;
    flex (rizz i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) {
        pool.es[i].x = 10.0;
        pool.es[i].y = 2.0;
        pool.es[i].alive = W;
    }
    pool.es[1].alive = L;

    scroll_all(&pool);
    flex (rizz i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) {
        yapping("es[%d] x=%.1f y=%.1f alive=%d",
                i, pool.es[i].x, pool.es[i].y, pool.es[i].alive);
    }
    🚽 neighbours intact after all that element traffic
    yapping("count=%d speed=%.1f", pool.count, pool.speed);

    🚽 two-dimensional struct array field
    gang Grid g;
    g.tag = 42;
    g.cells[0][0].v = 1;
    g.cells[1][2].v = 6;
    yapping("sizeof Grid: %lu", maxxing(g));
    yapping("grid %d %d tag=%d", g.cells[0][0].v, g.cells[1][2].v, g.tag);

    🚽 An ELEMENT of a struct-array field is a by-value struct value
    🚽 anywhere one is accepted. All such sites go through
    🚽 resolve_by_value_struct_source(), but they arrive with DIFFERENT
    🚽 LIFETIMES, so "by extension" is not a safe thing to assert -- an
    🚽 earlier version of this comment did, and the forms it waved at were
    🚽 exactly the ones that were broken (PR #315 review). Each is
    🚽 exercised explicitly below instead.
    pool.es[0].x = 3.0;
    pool.es[0].y = 4.0;
    yapping("len2 element: %.1f", len2(pool.es[0]));

    🚽 ... and the WHOLE array is not. Without this line a regression that
    🚽 silently passed es[0] again would go unnoticed -- it would print
    🚽 25.0 here instead of being refused.
    yapping("len2 whole:   %.1f", len2(pool.es));

    🚽 ── A CALL BASE: the shape where the lifetimes diverge ────────────
    🚽 `make_pool().es[0]` resolves through a base that points INTO the
    🚽 pending-return slot, which dies at the next
    🚽 free_pending_return_value(). Handing that back borrowed was a
    🚽 heap-use-after-free with two arguments (the second argument's own
    🚽 call freed the blob before the bind loop copied from it) and a leak
    🚽 with one. The plain-variable base above cannot catch it, because its
    🚽 blob lives in an ordinary variable that nothing frees mid-call.
    🚽
    🚽 All three consumer shapes are exercised, since it is precisely here
    🚽 that they stop being interchangeable: argument, two-argument
    🚽 (the UAF form), copy-initializer, and return.
    yapping("call arg:     %.1f", len2(make_pool().es[0]));
    yapping("call 2-arg:   %.1f", dot(make_pool().es[0], make_pool().es[1]));
    gang Entity ce = make_pool().es[1];
    yapping("call copyinit: %.1f", ce.x);
    yapping("call return:   %.1f", grab().x);

    🚽 The intersection with #316 (struct pointer arithmetic, which landed
    🚽 first): a pointer taken INTO a struct-array field, then walked. Each
    🚽 feature is tested on its own elsewhere; this is the shape neither
    🚽 fixture covers, and it is the one a real pool-walking helper writes.
    gang Entity *ep = &pool.es[0];
    yapping("ep    x=%.1f", ep.x);
    ep = ep + 2;
    yapping("ep+2  x=%.1f", ep.x);
    yapping("ep[0] x=%.1f", ep[0].x);

    🚽 a struct-array field nested two levels down, beside a by-value
    🚽 struct field of the same element type
    gang Outer o;
    o.m.one.a = 1.5;
    o.m.many[0].a = 2.5;
    o.m.many[1].a = 3.5;
    o.tag = 7;
    yapping("sizeof Outer: %lu", maxxing(o));
    yapping("nested %.1f %.1f %.1f tag=%d",
            o.m.one.a, o.m.many[0].a, o.m.many[1].a, o.tag);
    bussin 0;
}
